Rosin Fog Dub

Ingredients: KOREAN AJAENG + DUB TECHNO / BROKEN BEAT + PREPARED VIBRAPHONE + JANGGU RIM PERCUSSION + CONTRABASS CLARINET + FRETLESS ELECTRIC BASS

Creative Catalyst: The genre began during an autumn residency in a disused persimmon-sorting house outside Andong, where ajaeng player Seo Yuna was recording long low-register tones while London producer Malik Rowan tested sparse dub-techno chord loops through an old PA system. The discovery was not in the notes themselves but in the residue around them: rosin scrape before pitch, bridge grit during sustained pressure, brief string chatter when the bow changed direction, and the rough decay left when Yuna deliberately released pressure without muting the string. Malik stopped cleaning those sounds from the recordings. Instead, he separated pitch body and bow-noise residue into different delay paths, letting one musical gesture cast two different temporal shadows. Prepared vibraphone, janggu rim percussion, contrabass clarinet, and fretless bass completed a style where acoustic friction and dub echo coexist without either becoming background texture.

Rosin Fog Dub is a slow-to-midtempo acoustic-electronic form built from Korean ajaeng friction, dub-techno space, and broken-beat restraint. The groove sits between 108 and 120 BPM, but perceived motion is often slower because drums are deliberately incomplete. Deep compact kick, off-center rim hits, soft shuffled hats, muted broken-beat ghost notes, and short bass pulses create a floor that is stable enough for long bowed tones but porous enough for silence to remain structural.

The ajaeng is the principal voice, played in low and middle registers with sustained tones, narrow pitch bends, dry attacks, pressure swells, glissandi, open-string resonance, and exposed bow noise. Recording uses one close condenser above the bridge area for string definition, one ribbon microphone toward the body for low-mid weight, and one side-address condenser aimed at the bow contact zone specifically for scrape and friction. The friction microphone is not treated as an error channel. It is routed independently into short filtered delays and spring-like reflections while the pitched body stays comparatively dry.

The defining production idea is the Noise Tail: the audible pitch may stop while a filtered version of the bow scrape continues to repeat quietly in the stereo field. Conversely, a sustained pitch can remain nearly dry while a single rough attack creates several decaying rhythmic echoes. This separates articulation from tone and allows one acoustic gesture to occupy more than one timescale.

A second device, Pitch Dry / Friction Wet, keeps the fundamental ajaeng tone centered and intimate while sending only scrape, bridge noise, and direction-change residue into modulated delay. This prevents the instrument from dissolving into generic ambient reverb.

A third device, Empty Bar Pressure, deliberately removes kick and bass for one full bar while ajaeng, vibraphone, and friction echoes continue. The absence is not a breakdown or pre-drop build. The music simply loses its floor and lets the bowed instrument define time alone.

A fourth device, Echo Misregistration, offsets different delay returns by slightly unequal subdivisions. One friction echo may recur every dotted eighth, another every five sixteenths, while the dub chord delay remains on a conventional quarter-note or half-note grid. The resulting phase drift remains controlled because the dry drum pattern stays sparse.

Prepared vibraphone provides short, cold harmonic anchors. Selected bars are damped with removable felt or soft clips to shorten decay. Parts favor minor sevenths, suspended seconds, open fifths, minor sixth color, and two-note upper structures. Bowed-vibraphone swells are permitted only in sparse passages and never used as cinematic wash.

Janggu is reduced to dry rim, stick, and muted-head vocabulary rather than full traditional accompaniment. Short rim clicks, controlled low-head taps, and occasional split hand/stick accents replace selected machine percussion. The janggu should sound close, physical, and deliberately under-orchestrated.

Contrabass clarinet occupies the lowest acoustic register with chalumeau pedals, breath-only attacks, key clatter, slow falling seconds, tongue stops, and unstable air-to-tone transitions. It functions as a shadow rather than a bassline and is often heard when the fretless electric bass has disappeared.

Fretless electric bass uses very short root, fifth, minor-seventh, and octave phrases with soft finger attack and restrained slides. It does not imitate ajaeng bends. Its role is to stabilize the low register briefly, then leave enough room for acoustic resonance and delayed noise.

Dub-techno chords are minimal: one or two muted synth or processed electric-piano stabs every bar or two, filtered dark, with moderate tape delay and almost no bright top end. The chord layer must never become a constant pad. Space is created by omission, not by endless reverb.

Vocals are optional and sparse. Korean or English fragments may refer to sorting grades, fruit crates, cold storage, dust, fog, resin, late harvest, or phrases such as “leave the scrape,” “do not clean it,” “empty the floor,” and “let the noise return.” Delivery should be low, close, spoken, or narrow-range sung.

Effects remain disciplined. No huge shimmer, no cinematic Korean ensemble layers, no dubstep wobble bass, no festival risers, no long sub drops, no glossy trance pads, and no synthetic ajaeng doubling. The central contrast is intimate dry pitch against displaced, delayed friction.

Genre DNA

- Tempo & Pulse: 108–120 BPM, mostly 4/4 with broken-beat omissions, compact kick, dry rim/snare punctuation, restrained shuffled hats, short fretless-bass phrases, and frequent one-bar low-end vacancies.
- Harmonic Palette: Aeolian and Dorian centers, open fifths, suspended seconds, minor sevenths, minor-sixth color, static pedals, narrow pitch bends, and sparse two-chord fields.
- Production Signatures: Noise Tail; Pitch Dry / Friction Wet; Empty Bar Pressure; Echo Misregistration; close-miked janggu rim substitution; independent delay paths for pitch body versus bow noise.
- Dynamics: Intensity grows through denser friction echoes, wider ajaeng bow pressure, closer broken-beat ghost notes, stronger contrabass-clarinet air, and shorter gaps between bass entries. Climaxes remain murky, restrained, and low-density rather than loud.

Scene & Origin Lore

Rosin Fog Dub formed in a retired persimmon sorting and cold-storage cooperative outside Andong. The building retained wooden grading tables, metal fruit chutes, paper labels, old weighing scales, stacked plastic crates, fluorescent ceiling tubes, insulated cold-room doors, dust-coated fans, and handwritten harvest ledgers pinned beside conveyor controls.

Ajaeng player Seo Yuna had been using the empty grading hall because its dry acoustics made every rough bow contact audible. She initially considered the scraping transient a problem and repeatedly asked for cleaner takes. Producer Malik Rowan instead soloed the friction microphone and noticed that the scrape had a separate rhythmic identity from the pitched tone.

Prepared-vibraphone player Han Mira joined after devising short damped dyads that could sit between echo returns without filling the space. Percussionist Park Doyun reduced his setup to one janggu, two sticks, and a small hardwood block. Contrabass-clarinetist Ellis Crane completed the ensemble with low breath and key noise for sections where the electronic bass vanished.

Their first rehearsals were called fog takes because condensation from the old cold rooms drifted into the warmer sorting hall during early-morning sessions. The name persisted even after the refrigeration system was permanently shut down.

The visual language developed around persimmon orange, cold-room white, crate grey, dark lacquered ajaeng wood, faded green grading labels, dust brown, and weak fluorescent blue. The unofficial rule is that if the mix sounds cleaner when the bow noise is removed, the composition was not built specifically enough around the instrument.

