Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Grade B Fruit Under Fluorescent Fog
- Story & Context: At 114 BPM, Seo Yuna opens with one low ajaeng tone played nearly dry while Malik routes only the rough bow attack into a dotted-eighth filtered delay. The kick enters on beats one and three, but the snare is replaced every second bar by a janggu rim click. Han Mira adds one damped vibraphone minor-seventh dyad after the third friction echo, never on the initial attack. Midway through, Empty Bar Pressure removes kick and fretless bass for a full bar while the original ajaeng pitch has already stopped; only the Noise Tail, vibraphone decay, and low contrabass-clarinet air remain. The groove returns quietly with no riser.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Fluorescent-lit sorting table with three bruised orange persimmons labeled GRADE B, pale condensation hovering above grey crates, thin waveform-like scrape marks drawn across a harvest card. Sparse lyric fragments: “grade B,” “leave the scrape,” “fog under light,” “do not clean it.”

Track 2: The Cold Room Keeps the Wrong Echo
- Story & Context: At 118 BPM, the track uses Echo Misregistration as its main architecture. Seo plays short mid-register ajaeng phrases while two friction-only delay paths recur at unequal rhythmic intervals, causing their returns to drift relative to the dry beat. Malik keeps the dub chord on a conventional half-note echo, creating a stable reference against the moving scrape. Park Doyun uses muted janggu low-head taps only when both friction delays momentarily align. Ellis alternates fretless bass and contrabass clarinet so the low register changes material without increasing density. The final section removes the chord stab entirely, leaving dry ajaeng pitch against misregistered scrape echoes.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Open cold-room door with condensation on the floor, two misaligned harvest labels stamped with different times, one dark ajaeng string diagram crossing both. Sparse lyric fragments: “wrong echo,” “cold room,” “two clocks,” “pitch stays here.”

Track 3: Last Crate After the Fan Stops
- Story & Context: The closer runs at 108 BPM and begins without kick. Yuna plays isolated bowed tones separated by long silence, each with a different ratio of dry pitch to wet friction. Han uses only two prepared-vibraphone bars, struck softly after selected bow releases. Park contributes occasional woodblock and janggu rim taps as if marking inventory rather than playing a groove. Ellis holds unstable contrabass-clarinet air notes beneath the longest silences. In the final minute, the old-room fan sound recorded during the session is left audible as Malik removes every electronic layer. One final ajaeng attack produces a long Noise Tail after the pitch itself has vanished. The track ends when the delay decays below the room noise rather than on a final chord.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: One remaining grey fruit crate under a stopped industrial fan, harvest ledger closed beside a single dark-orange persimmon, faint condensation and dust in a weak fluorescent beam. Sparse lyric fragments: “last crate,” “fan stopped,” “pitch gone,” “noise remains.”

